{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nTwo operator-facing messages in `src/rebar/_store/env_identity.py` still say that a tracker re-clone makes the old environment's op-cert attestations unverifiable, with a `foreign_key` verdict at the claim and close gates, and that they \"must be re-earned\":\n\n- the `ENV_ID_MISMATCH` fsck line returned by `divergence_report` (`env_identity.py:268-277`);\n- the mint warning returned by `reidentification_warning` (`env_identity.py:163-177`).\n\nSince commit `3bde5613718` (bug `c21f-6f29-5d2d-4a5a`, \"Stop gating op-certs on signing environment\") that is false by default. `_opcert_trust_root` (`src/rebar/_opcert_verify.py:113-140`) falls back from the own key to a pinned key to the public key embedded in the SSHSIG envelope (`trust_basis: envelope_key`), so a cert signed under a lost identity still certifies. It returns no key, and the cert turns `foreign_key`, only when `verify.require_environment` is set (`_opcert_verify.py:134-135` and `:239-249`). That is pinned by `test_cross_environment_cert_certifies_when_enforcement_unset` and `test_enforcement_on_withholds_the_envelope_key_fallback` in `tests/unit/test_opcert_cross_environment_c21f.py`. `docs/manifest-signing.md:42`, `docs/reuse-surface.md:137` and `src/rebar/schemas/verify_signature_result.schema.json` already describe `foreign_key` that way.\n\nAn operator who reads the messages re-runs plan-review and completion gates that do not need it.\n\nA string grep for the old claim found it in 3 more places that the original description missed:\n\n- the `env_identity.py` module docstring (lines 3-7: \"attestations from another environment do not verify here ... store-wide `foreign_key` verdict\") and the `reidentification_warning` docstring (line 154: \"has just orphaned its own attestations\");\n- `docs/concurrency.md` §\"Re-cloning the tracker\" (lines 1030-1089), which contradicts its own closing paragraph (line 1081, \"A re-clone no longer invalidates op-cert attestations\"): line 1044 (\"losing it fails **silently and late**\"), lines 1056-1060 (\"has just orphaned that environment's attestations\"), and lines 1076-1077 (\"discovering the loss one ticket at a time at a gate\").\n\n## Approach\n\nReword each site to the behaviour `_opcert_trust_root` implements, using the closing paragraph of `docs/concurrency.md` (line 1081) as the reference text, because it is already correct and is the canonical operator doc. Message wording:\n\n- Mint warning, re-clone branch: the old identity's attestations still certify here on the public key embedded in each signature; they turn `foreign_key` only under the opt-in `verify.require_environment`, which trusts only that environment's own or pinned keys. A re-clone costs one continuous audit identity and the ability to sign as the old identity. The carry-over list and the `REBAR_ALLOW_ENV_REIDENTIFY` line stay unchanged.\n- `ENV_ID_MISMATCH` line: the same two facts replace \"cannot be verified here ... must be re-earned\". The overlap wording from Gerrit 3270 (`7ea86184dac`) and the carry-over names stay unchanged.\n\nIn `docs/concurrency.md`, the re-clone bullet keeps its recovery advice and says to restore `.env-id` together with `.opcert-key`. With the old id but a newly minted key, a cert signed under the old key is checked against the new key and fails as `mismatch` unless that key is pinned (`test_cert_under_an_unpinned_previous_key_still_fails`, `tests/unit/test_opcert_own_principal_rotation_d909.py:174`).\n\nNo verifier behaviour changes; this is text only.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_store/env_identity.py`: 2 message strings and 2 docstrings.\n- `docs/concurrency.md`: the prose of §\"Re-cloning the tracker\" (lines 1030-1089). Edit the paragraph at lines 1044-1048, the re-clone bullet at lines 1056-1060, and the sentence at lines 1076-1077. The carry-over table and the closing paragraph at line 1081 stay as they are.\n- `tests/interfaces/store/test_env_identity_guard.py`: new assertions on both messages.\n\n## Testing\n\n- New tests in `tests/interfaces/store/test_env_identity_guard.py`, written first and failing on `origin/main`:\n  - `test_reidentification_warning_says_old_attestations_still_certify`: the unacknowledged warning names `verify.require_environment` and `foreign_key`, and contains neither \"unverifiable\" nor \"re-earned\".\n  - `test_divergence_report_says_old_attestations_still_certify`: the `ENV_ID_MISMATCH` line built from overlapping spans makes the same assertions.\n- Edge: the acknowledged `NOTE:` form is unchanged; the existing `test_override_acknowledges_and_quietens_the_warning` still passes.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Neither message claims that attestations become unverifiable by default, and both name `verify.require_environment` as the condition for `foreign_key`; verified by the 2 new tests passing in `pytest tests/interfaces/store/test_env_identity_guard.py`, which fail on `origin/main`.\n- [ ] The existing tests still pass: `pytest tests/interfaces/store/test_env_identity_guard.py tests/unit/test_opcert_cross_environment_c21f.py tests/unit/test_opcert_own_principal_rotation_d909.py` exits 0.\n- [ ] No old-claim phrase remains: `git grep -nF -e 're-earned' -e 'unverifiable here' -e 'cannot be verified here' -e 'do not verify here' -e 'orphaned its own attestations' -e 'orphaned that' -e 'silently and late' -e 'one ticket at a time at a gate' -- src/rebar/_store/env_identity.py docs/concurrency.md` prints nothing. Worked example: on `origin/main` (`c6f116f1ae8`) the same command with `-c` prints `docs/concurrency.md:3` (lines 1044, 1056, 1077) and `src/rebar/_store/env_identity.py:6` (lines 5, 154, 170-171, 273-274); after the change both counts are absent.\n- [ ] `make lint` and `make typecheck` exit 0 with the worktree `.venv/bin` first on `PATH`."}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1793754187908803001,"uuid":"ef7f1a80-4935-4e20-9b41-5f8a520868a3"}